org.springframework.context.annotation.ConflictingBeanDefinitionException:
Annotation-specified bean name 'counterController' for bean class [com.dxc.remoteSeaProbe.test.CounterController]
conflicts with existing, non-compatible bean definition of same name and class [com.dxc.remoteSeaProbe.sample.CounterController]

Build passes (because compilation is fine)

Runtime fails (because Spring context loads beans)
💥 Boom — runtime failure.

| Phase               | What happens                   |
| ------------------- | ------------------------------ |
| `mvn clean install` | Just compiles classes          |
| Spring Boot startup | Creates beans + scans packages |

Bean conflicts are runtime-only problems.
This is why seniors never trust build success alone.

Option 1 (BEST PRACTICE): Delete test/sample controller

If this is NOT needed in prod:

com.dxc.remoteSeaProbe.test.CounterController
or
com.dxc.remoteSeaProbe.sample.CounterController
👉 Delete it
👉 Or move it to src/test/java

✅ Clean
✅ Professional
✅ No hacks

This is what I would do immediately.

🥈 Option 2: Exclude test/sample package from component scan

If you want to keep it for reference:

@SpringBootApplication
@ComponentScan(
    basePackages = "com.dxc.remoteSeaProbe",
    excludeFilters = @ComponentScan.Filter(
        type = FilterType.REGEX,
        pattern = "com.dxc.remoteSeaProbe.test.*"
    )
)
public class RemoteSeaProbeApplication {}
👍 Good
👎 Slightly noisy config

Option 3: Explicit bean names (NOT recommended long-term)
@RestController("counterControllerTest")
public class CounterController {}
And:

@RestController("counterControllerSample")
public class CounterController {}

⚠️ This fixes the symptom, not the problem
⚠️ I don’t recommend this for controllers
